2.6 Impossibility of Oscillations
Fixed points dominate the dynamics of first-order systems. In all our examples so far, all trajectories either approached a fixed point, or diverged to od. In fact, those are the only things that can happen for a vector field on the real line. The reason is that trajectories are forced to increase or decrease monotonically, or remain constant (Figure 2.6.1). To put it more geometrically, the phase point never reverses direction.


